<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Province of Albacete</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Province of Albacete is a province of Castilla–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, with its capital at the city of Albacete. It was created in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javier-de-burgos">Javier de Burgos</a>'s territorial division of 1833 from parts of the old Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> and La Mancha. It belonged to the historical region of Murcia until it joined Castilla–La Mancha in 1982.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/province-of-albacete.jpg" alt="Province of Albacete" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albacete_in_Spain_(plus_Canarias).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Province of Albacete lies in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, in the southern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a>, in the southeast of the Central Plateau. Its capital, the city of Albacete, is 262 kilometres by road southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. The province borders the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, Cuenca, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-real">Ciudad Real</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a>. Its main river is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jucar">Júcar</a>, which crosses the northeast and is known for its deep gorges and trees. The highest point is La Atalaya, at 2,083 metres in the Sierra de las Cabras.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory has been inhabited since ancient times, as cave paintings in the Cueva del Niño and the Cueva de la Vieja show. In Roman times it was divided among Carpetania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celtiberia">Celtiberia</a>, Contestania, Bastetania and Oretania. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a significant settlement at Libisosa, and under the Visigoths Tolmo de Minateda grew in prominence. In the Muslim period numerous farmhouses, castles and watchtowers were built to fight off invaders. On 25 April 1707 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa">Battle of Almansa</a> was fought near Albacete, in which Philip V triumphed and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a> on the Spanish throne.</p><p>In 1822 the short-lived Province of Chinchilla was created from municipalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a>, Cuenca and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, with its capital at Chinchilla de Monte-Aragón, very close to Albacete. It became the basis on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javier-de-burgos">Javier de Burgos</a> created the Province of Albacete in 1833. After the Constitution of 1978 the province left the Murcia region and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>–La Mancha, whose Statute of Autonomy was approved in 1982.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The province covers 14,931.74 square kilometres and has 87 municipalities and seven judicial districts, with a population of 390,751. Besides the capital, towns of more than 10,000 inhabitants are Hellín, Villarrobledo, Almansa, La Roda and Caudete.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Province of Albacete” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1833 Javier de Burgos's reform divided Spain into provinces, splitting the old kingdom into the provinces of Murcia and <b>Albacete</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-murcia">Kingdom of Murcia</a></li></ul></section>
